%% DM26-0670

%% A delivery from the broker, as built (2026-11-04, docs/architecture/cassini-client-reliability.md, "Step 5's acknowledged deliveries, as built"). At-least-once:
%% acknowledged on hand-off to the router, not after the agent's work, and a repeat is not dropped. Shaded is the design, not built (steps 4 and 5a).
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Broker (the subscriber's delivery outbox)
    participant L as its Listener
    participant T as Transport (reader, acknowledgement writer)
    participant R as Router
    participant D as Domain actor

    B->>B: number it and hold it until it is acknowledged
    B->>L: PushMessage(delivery_id)
    L->>T: PublishResponse(topic, payload, published_by, published_instance, origin epoch and seq, delivery_id)
    alt the frame does not decode
        T->>L: FrameUndecodable(frame), and the delivery it carried is let go as acknowledged
    else
        rect rgb(235, 219, 178)
        Note over T,R: DESIGNED, step 4: origin (publisher, epoch, seq) already seen? Skip it and acknowledge again.
        end
        T->>R: IncomingMessage(topic, payload, published_by)
        R->>D: the topic's registered handler, into its mailbox
        T->>L: PublishResponseAck(delivery_id), by the writer's own task
        L->>B: PublishResponseAck(delivery_id), to that topic's subscriber of its session
        B->>B: remove it from the outbox, and its backlog falls
        rect rgb(235, 219, 178)
        Note over D,B: DESIGNED, step 5a: acknowledge after the agent's work, such as a consumer's graph write. Until then,<br/>a consumer that dies with a message in its mailbox loses it.
        end
    end
    Note over B,T: If the connection fails before the acknowledgement, the broker keeps the delivery and sends it again<br/>on resume with the number it had, so one the client had may arrive twice, and the agent sees both.
